    prerequisites: ["Introduction"],
    concept: "Think of learning to cook: you CAN cook over an open fire with a single knife, but a proper kitchen (stove, oven, sharp knives, a fridge) makes the same recipes dramatically faster and less error-prone - it doesn't change WHAT you're cooking, only how comfortably you get there. Setting up your Java environment is exactly this: installing the JDK gives you the raw tools (javac to compile, java to run), and an IDE (IntelliJ IDEA, Eclipse, VS Code with Java extensions) is the fully-equipped kitchen - auto-complete, instant error highlighting, one-click run, integrated debugging.\n\nConcretely: install a JDK (a recent LTS version like 17 or 21 is the standard choice today), then confirm it worked by running `java -version` and `javac -version` in a terminal - both must report a version, not 'command not found'. Every Java program starts life as a `.java` text file; `javac` compiles it into a `.class` bytecode file; `java` then runs that bytecode on the JVM. Doing this once by hand on the command line - before ever touching an IDE's 'Run' button - is genuinely worth it: it makes visible exactly what the IDE is quietly automating for you, so when something goes wrong later, you're not staring at a black box.",
