    goingDeeper: "Java has continued evolving well past Java 8 on a faster 6-month release cadence (since Java 9) - records (Java 16, a compact way to declare an immutable data-holder class), sealed classes (Java 17, restricting exactly which classes may extend/implement a given type), and pattern matching for switch (Java 21, mentioned in the Conditional Statements lesson) are the most significant recent additions worth being AWARE of, even if this path focuses primarily on the still-foundational Java 8 feature set most real-world enterprise codebases are built on today.",
