    whatYoullLearn: ["How product-company Java interview questions differ in depth/flavor from placement-drive questions", "A rapid-fire review of the highest-frequency Java concepts across this entire language path", "How to structure a strong Java interview answer"],
    prerequisites: ["Multithreading", "Streams", "Design Patterns", "Memory Management"],
    concept: "If placement-drive questions (the previous lesson) are the driving test, product-company technical interviews are closer to a real road trip with an experienced co-driver watching how you actually handle unexpected turns - they go deeper, ask WHY more often, and frequently combine several topics into ONE scenario rather than testing each in isolation.\n\nA strong Java interview answer follows the same three-part shape used consistently across this course's other subjects: precise definition, a concrete example, and the key trade-off or distinction from something commonly confused with it. Asked 'explain the difference between an interface and an abstract class', a strong answer states the precise definitions, gives a concrete Shape/Vehicle-style example, AND states the actual decision criterion (shared implementation code favors abstract class; contract-only across otherwise-unrelated classes favors interface) - not just the bare definitions alone.\n\nThe handful of topics worth having rock-solid, ready to go, because they appear across nearly every Java interview regardless of company or role: == vs .equals(), checked vs unchecked exceptions, the Stack vs Heap distinction (and what causes each kind of memory error), how HashMap actually works internally (hashing into buckets, and what happens on a collision), start() vs run() for threads, and at least two design patterns with a genuine real-world example each, not just their names.",
    keyPoints: ["Product-company interviews go deeper and combine topics more than placement-drive questions - expect 'why' follow-ups, not just recall", "Strong answer structure: precise definition, concrete example, AND the key trade-off/distinction from something commonly confused with it", "Highest-frequency topics worth having rock-solid: == vs .equals(), checked/unchecked exceptions, Stack vs Heap, HashMap internals, start() vs run(), and 2+ design patterns with real examples"],
    commonMistakes: ["Answering only with a memorized definition when the interviewer clearly wants the REASONING behind it (the 'why', the trade-off) - depth of understanding, not recall alone, is what's actually being evaluated at this level", "Being unable to connect two related topics when asked a combined question (e.g. 'how does garbage collection interact with a Singleton that never gets dereferenced?') - practicing topics only in isolation leaves this kind of connective-reasoning question exposed"],
    interviewTips: ["Practice explaining EVERY item in this lesson's 'rock-solid' list out loud, in under 60 seconds each, with a concrete example - not just silently reading and nodding along in agreement", "When you genuinely don't know something, say precisely what you DO know and reason toward the rest out loud - this consistently scores better than guessing silently or freezing entirely"],
    realWorldApplications: ["The exact same 'definition, example, trade-off' answer structure used throughout this entire course (CS Core's OS/DBMS/OOP interview-prep lessons included) transfers directly here too - it's one genuinely reusable interview skill, not something unique to Java specifically"],
