    concept: "C++ was created by Bjarne Stroustrup in 1985 as 'C with Classes' - literally starting as C, then layering object-oriented programming, templates, and a rich standard library on top. This history matters practically: almost all valid C code is also valid C++ (with minor exceptions), so everything you already know about variables, loops, arrays, and pointers carries over directly - C++ adds capability, it doesn't replace what you learned.\n\nThe single biggest reason C++ dominates competitive programming and DSA interview rounds specifically is the STL (Standard Template Library) - ready-made, highly optimized data structures (vector, map, set, priority_queue) and algorithms (sort, binary_search) that let you focus on problem-solving logic instead of re-implementing a dynamic array or a hash map from scratch every single time, unlike plain C.\n\nA minimal C++ program looks almost identical to C, but uses `#include <iostream>` and `std::cout`/`std::cin` for input/output instead of C's printf/scanf (though C's printf/scanf still work fine in C++ too) - `std::cout << \"Hello\" << std::endl;` chains output using the `<<` operator rather than a format-string.",
