    concept: "C++'s basic shape mirrors C exactly: a required `main()` function, semicolon-terminated statements, and `#include` directives - but C++ introduces NAMESPACES, a way to group names (functions, classes, variables) to avoid naming collisions between different libraries. The entire C++ standard library lives inside the `std` namespace, which is why you either write `std::cout` explicitly or add `using namespace std;` once at the top to avoid typing `std::` before every standard library name.\n\n`cout` (console output) and `cin` (console input) use operator OVERLOADING (a C++ OOP feature covered later) to repurpose `<<` (normally 'bitwise left shift') and `>>` (normally 'bitwise right shift') as 'send this value to' and 'read a value from' - `cout << \"Age: \" << age << endl;` chains multiple outputs together in one statement, something C's printf can't do as naturally.\n\n`endl` both prints a newline AND flushes the output buffer (forcing it to actually display immediately); `\"\\n\"` just prints a newline without flushing - in performance-sensitive competitive programming, `\"\\n\"` is often preferred over `endl` specifically because repeated flushing has a real, measurable cost across many output calls.",
