    whatYoullLearn: ["Reading and writing with cin/cout in more depth", "Reading a full line with getline", "Why cin >> stops at whitespace, and when that's a problem"],
    prerequisites: ["Operators"],
    concept: "`cin >> variable` reads input but stops at the first WHITESPACE (space, tab, or newline) - perfectly fine for reading a single word or number, but a common surprise when reading a full name like 'Priya Sharma': `cin >> name;` would only capture 'Priya', silently leaving 'Sharma' unread in the input buffer.\n\n`getline(cin, variable)` (from `<string>`) reads an ENTIRE line, including spaces, up to the next newline - the correct choice whenever you need a full line of text rather than a single token. A classic C++ gotcha: mixing `cin >> x;` followed by `getline(cin, line);` often 'skips' the getline unexpectedly, because the `>>` operator leaves the trailing newline character still sitting in the input buffer, which getline then immediately consumes as an empty line - the fix is usually an extra `cin.ignore();` between the two calls to discard that leftover newline first.\n\nChaining multiple `cin >>` reads in one statement (`cin >> a >> b >> c;`) works because `>>` (like `<<`) returns a reference to `cin` itself, letting the calls chain left-to-right.",
    keyPoints: ["cin >> stops reading at the first whitespace - use it for single words/numbers, not full lines with spaces", "getline(cin, str) reads an entire line including spaces - the right tool for full-text input", "Mixing cin >> then getline commonly 'skips' the getline due to a leftover newline - cin.ignore() fixes it", "cin >> a >> b chains because >> returns a reference to cin, allowing left-to-right chaining"],
    commonMistakes: ["Using cin >> to read a full name or sentence and being confused when only the first word is captured", "Mixing cin >> and getline without cin.ignore() in between, causing the getline call to appear to 'do nothing'"],
    interviewTips: ["Be ready to explain and fix the classic 'getline seems to be skipped' bug - a very commonly encountered real beginner issue worth understanding precisely, not just patching by trial and error"],
    realWorldApplications: ["Correctly reading mixed numeric and full-line text input is a frequent, practical requirement in real command-line tools and competitive programming input parsing"],
    codeExample: { language: "cpp", code: "#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    int age;\n    string fullName;\n    cout << \"Enter age: \";\n    cin >> age;\n    cin.ignore();  // discard the leftover newline from the >> read\n    cout << \"Enter full name: \";\n    getline(cin, fullName);\n    cout << fullName << \" is \" << age << \" years old.\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}" },
