  "loops": {
    difficulty: "Beginner", estimatedMinutes: 20,
    whatYoullLearn: ["for, while, do-while - identical to C", "The range-based for loop, a genuine C++ addition", "Why range-based for is preferred when iterating a whole container"],
    prerequisites: ["Conditional Statements"],
    concept: "C++'s classic loops (`for`, `while`, `do-while`) behave exactly like C's - everything from the previous course's Loops lesson transfers directly, including the risk of off-by-one boundary errors. What C++ adds is the RANGE-BASED FOR LOOP (introduced in C++11): `for (int x : myArray) { ... }` iterates over every element of a container or array directly, without manually managing an index variable at all.\n\nThis matters for two real reasons: it's harder to get an off-by-one wrong since there's no manual index to mismanage, and it works uniformly across arrays, STL vectors, strings, and other containers you'll meet soon - the same clean syntax regardless of what you're iterating over. Using `for (int& x : myArray)` (a REFERENCE, `&`) instead of `for (int x : myArray)` lets you MODIFY the original elements while iterating, since `x` then refers directly to each element rather than holding a copy of it.\n\nRange-based for is the modern, preferred style whenever you genuinely need EVERY element in order with no need for the index itself - reach for a classic indexed for loop only when you actually need the index (like printing 'item #3: ...') or need to iterate in a non-sequential pattern.",
    keyPoints: ["Classic for/while/do-while behave identically to C, including off-by-one risk", "Range-based for (for (auto x : container)) is a C++-only addition that iterates every element without a manual index", "for (auto& x : container) uses a reference, letting you modify elements in place during iteration", "Use range-based for when you need every element in order and don't need the index itself"],
    commonMistakes: ["Using for (auto x : container) when you actually need to modify elements - without the & reference, x is just a copy and changes don't affect the original", "Reaching for a classic indexed loop out of habit when range-based for would be clearer and safer"],
    interviewTips: ["Be ready to explain precisely when a reference (&) is needed in a range-based for loop versus when a plain copy is fine (read-only iteration)"],
    realWorldApplications: ["Range-based for loops are the standard, idiomatic way modern C++ code iterates over vectors, strings, and other STL containers throughout real codebases"],
    codeExample: { language: "cpp", code: "#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    int scores[5] = {85, 90, 78, 92, 88};\n\n    // range-based for: read-only\n    for (int score : scores) {\n        cout << score << \" \";\n    }\n    cout << \"\\n\";\n\n    // range-based for with reference: modifies in place\n    for (int& score : scores) {\n        score += 5;  // curve every score up by 5\n    }\n    for (int score : scores) cout << score << \" \";\n    cout << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}" },
    mcqs: [
      { question: "What C++-specific loop lets you iterate every element of a container without managing an index?", options: ["do-while", "The range-based for loop", "A goto loop", "while(true)"], correctIndex: 1 },
      { question: "Why would you use `for (int& x : arr)` instead of `for (int x : arr)`?", options: ["No real difference", "The reference (&) lets you modify the original array elements during iteration", "It's required syntax", "It makes the loop run faster in every case"], correctIndex: 1 },
    ],
    assignment: "Write a program that uses a range-based for loop with a reference to double every element of an integer array in place, then prints the modified array using a second range-based for loop (read-only this time).",
    goingDeeper: "Range-based for loops work on ANY type that provides begin()/end() methods (the 'iterator' pattern) - this is exactly why they work uniformly across arrays, std::vector, std::string, std::map, and even your own custom classes later, once you implement that same begin()/end() interface yourself.",
    xpReward: 25, coinReward: 10,
  },
