    prerequisites: ["Loops"],
    concept: "C-style fixed-size arrays (`int arr[5];`) work identically in C++ to C - same zero-based indexing, same lack of automatic bounds checking, same fixed size. But C++ also gives you `std::vector<int>` (from `<vector>`) - a DYNAMIC array that can grow and shrink at runtime, automatically manages its own memory (no manual malloc/free needed), and knows its own current size via `.size()`.\n\n`vector<int> nums;` starts empty; `nums.push_back(10);` appends an element, automatically growing the underlying storage as needed; `nums[i]` accesses an element by index just like a raw array (still with NO bounds checking, matching raw array behavior - use `.at(i)` instead if you specifically want an exception thrown on an out-of-range access). For nearly all real C++ code and competitive programming, `vector` is preferred over a raw array specifically because it eliminates fixed-size limitations and manual memory management entirely, at effectively no meaningful performance cost.\n\nThis is a genuine, important habit shift: where C forced you to think carefully about array sizing upfront, C++'s vector lets you build up a collection dynamically as you go, which matches how most real problems (reading an unknown number of inputs, building a result list) actually shape up.",
    keyPoints: ["C-style arrays (int arr[5]) work identically to C - same indexing, same lack of bounds checking, fixed size", "std::vector<int> is a dynamic array: grows automatically via push_back(), tracks its own size via .size()", "vector[i] has no bounds checking (like a raw array); vector.at(i) throws an exception on out-of-range access instead", "vector is preferred over raw arrays in nearly all real C++ code, for safety and flexibility with no meaningful performance cost"],
    commonMistakes: ["Using a fixed-size raw array when the needed size isn't known upfront, instead of just using a vector", "Forgetting that vector[i] (like a raw array) does NOT bounds-check - only .at(i) does"],
    interviewTips: ["Be ready to explain why vector is generally preferred over a raw C-style array in modern C++ - dynamic sizing and automatic memory management are the key reasons", "Know the size()/push_back()/at() vector methods, since they appear constantly in DSA solutions"],
    realWorldApplications: ["std::vector is the single most commonly used container across all real-world C++ code and virtually every competitive-programming/DSA solution you'll write in C++"],
    codeExample: { language: "cpp", code: "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    vector<int> scores;\n    scores.push_back(85);\n    scores.push_back(90);\n    scores.push_back(78);\n\n    for (int s : scores) cout << s << \" \";\n    cout << \"\\nSize: \" << scores.size() << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}" },
