    prerequisites: ["Strings"],
    concept: "C++ functions work exactly like C's, but add two real capabilities. First, DEFAULT ARGUMENTS: `void greet(string name, string greeting = \"Hello\") { ... }` lets a caller omit the last argument (`greet(\"Priya\");` uses the default greeting) - impossible in plain C, where every argument is always required.\n\nSecond, and much more significant: PASS-BY-REFERENCE using `&` in the parameter list, a direct, cleaner replacement for C's pointer-based workaround: `void increment(int &x) { x++; }` called as `increment(age);` (no `&` needed at the CALL site, unlike C's pointer approach) directly modifies the caller's original variable - references read like plain variables inside the function body (no `*` dereferencing needed) while still achieving the same 'modify the original' effect pointers required in C.\n\nThird, FUNCTION OVERLOADING: C++ lets you define multiple functions with the SAME name but different parameter types/counts (`int add(int a, int b)` and `double add(double a, double b)`) - the compiler picks the right one based on the arguments you actually pass, something C doesn't support at all (C requires every function name to be globally unique).",
    keyPoints: ["Default arguments let a caller omit trailing parameters, using a specified default value instead", "Pass-by-reference (int &x) is C++'s cleaner alternative to C's pointer-based 'modify the caller's variable' pattern - no & at the call site, no * inside the function", "Function overloading lets multiple functions share a name with different parameter types/counts - the compiler picks the right one automatically", "None of these three capabilities (default args, references, overloading) exist in plain C"],
    commonMistakes: ["Confusing a reference parameter (int &x) with a pointer parameter (int *x) - references need no & at the call site and no * to use, unlike pointers", "Defining overloaded functions that are ambiguous for certain calls (like overloads that could both match equally well), causing a compile error"],
    interviewTips: ["Be ready to explain precisely how references differ from pointers - references cannot be reseated (rebound to point elsewhere after initialization) or be null, unlike pointers, which is exactly why they're considered 'safer'"],
