    keyPoints: ["A mini-project combines OOP (classes, inheritance, polymorphism), STL containers, and smart pointers into ONE working system", "Three right-sized ideas: Library Management System, Shape Area Calculator, Contact Manager - each forces real STL-container and ownership-model design decisions", "Build the smallest working version first, then layer on features incrementally, exactly like the C mini-projects approach"],
    commonMistakes: ["Reaching for raw new/delete or raw pointers instead of the smart pointers and STL containers this entire C++ path has specifically taught", "Skipping the polymorphic design (using a switch on a 'type' field instead of virtual functions) when the whole point is practicing what was actually taught"],
    interviewTips: ["A finished project using unique_ptr, STL containers, and polymorphism together is genuinely strong, concrete interview material - being able to explain WHY you chose map over vector, or unique_ptr over shared_ptr, for your specific project demonstrates real understanding, not just recall"],
