    prerequisites: ["Syntax"],
    concept: "JavaScript has three variable-declaration keywords, and understanding WHY there are three (not just what each does) is genuinely important: `var` (the original, from JavaScript's very first version) is FUNCTION-scoped, meaning a `var` declared inside an `if` block or `for` loop is still accessible OUTSIDE that block, anywhere in the enclosing function - a real source of confusing bugs, since it doesn't match how most other languages' block scoping works.\n\n`let` and `const` (added in ES6/2015) fix this with proper BLOCK scope - a `let`/`const` declared inside `{ }` (an if block, a loop, any braces) is only accessible WITHIN that block, matching the intuitive scoping behavior you'd expect from C/Java/Python. `let` allows reassignment (`let count = 0; count++;`); `const` does NOT allow reassignment after initialization (`const MAX = 100;` then `MAX = 200;` is a runtime error) - though importantly, `const` only prevents reassigning the VARIABLE itself, not mutating an object/array it points to (`const arr = [1,2]; arr.push(3);` is completely valid - you're mutating the array's contents, not reassigning `arr` itself).\n\nThe modern, universally recommended practice: use `const` by DEFAULT for everything, switch to `let` only when you genuinely need to reassign the variable later, and avoid `var` entirely in new code - this default-to-const habit also makes code easier to reason about, since seeing `const` immediately tells a reader 'this binding never changes'.",
    keyPoints: ["var is function-scoped (leaks out of if/for blocks) - a legacy behavior avoided in modern code", "let and const are block-scoped, matching intuitive C/Java/Python-style scoping", "const prevents reassigning the variable itself, but does NOT prevent mutating an object/array it points to", "Modern practice: default to const, use let only when reassignment is genuinely needed, avoid var entirely"],
    commonMistakes: ["Using var out of old habit or tutorials, then being confused when a variable 'leaks' outside its if/for block", "Assuming const makes an object or array fully immutable - it only prevents reassigning the variable binding itself, not mutating the object's contents"],
