    concept: "JavaScript has only ONE numeric type, `number`, representing BOTH integers and decimals (unlike C's separate int/float/double) - `5` and `5.5` are both just `number` under the hood (a 64-bit floating point representation), which is convenient but does mean floating-point precision quirks (like `0.1 + 0.2` not exactly equaling `0.3`) apply to ALL numbers, even ones that look like plain integers.\n\nOther primitives: `string` (text, in single, double, OR backtick quotes), `boolean` (true/false), `undefined` (a variable that's been DECLARED but never ASSIGNED a value), and `null` (an explicit, deliberate 'no value' that a programmer assigns on purpose) - the distinction matters: `undefined` usually means 'this was never set', `null` means 'this was deliberately set to nothing'. `typeof` checks a value's type at runtime: `typeof 5` is `\"number\"`, `typeof \"hi\"` is `\"string\"` - though famously, `typeof null` returns `\"object\"`, a decades-old bug baked permanently into the language for backward-compatibility reasons.\n\nJavaScript is DYNAMICALLY typed - a variable's type isn't fixed at declaration; `let x = 5; x = \"now a string\";` is completely valid, unlike C's static typing - genuinely convenient for quick scripts, but a real source of subtle bugs in larger programs if types aren't tracked carefully (this exact problem is precisely what TypeScript, covered later in this path, was created to solve).",
