    concept: "Placement and screening rounds for frontend/full-stack roles favor a genuinely different flavor of 'predict the output' question than C/C++ rounds, exploiting JavaScript's own distinctive quirks instead: type coercion with `==` (`\"5\" == 5` questions), truthy/falsy surprises (`Boolean([])` being true), var's function-scope leak versus let/const's block scope, and `this` behaving differently between regular functions and arrow functions.\n\nA recurring, genuinely tricky category: closures inside loops - `for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) { setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 100); }` prints `3, 3, 3` (since `var` is function-scoped, all three callbacks share the SAME `i`, which has already reached 3 by the time any of them run), while replacing `var` with `let` prints `0, 1, 2` (since `let` creates a NEW block-scoped binding for each loop iteration) - a classic, extremely commonly asked JavaScript-specific gotcha question that doesn't have a direct equivalent in the C/C++ path at all.\n\nA genuinely effective prep strategy: practice tracing through short snippets combining hoisting, `this`, closures, and type coercion BY HAND first, since these interact in ways that are easy to get wrong purely by intuition without carefully tracing the actual execution order.",
