    whatYoullLearn: ["Installing the TypeScript compiler (tsc)", "Compiling a .ts file to .js and running it", "tsconfig.json's basic purpose"],
    prerequisites: ["Introduction"],
    concept: "TypeScript itself is installed via npm (Node's package manager): `npm install -g typescript` installs the `tsc` (TypeScript Compiler) command globally. `tsc filename.ts` compiles a `.ts` file into a corresponding `.js` file in the same location, which you then run normally with `node filename.js` - two separate steps (compile, then run), similar in spirit to C/C++'s compile-then-run workflow, just for a genuinely different reason (type erasure and syntax transformation, rather than producing native machine code).\n\nFor any real project, a `tsconfig.json` file configures the compiler's behavior project-wide (which JS version to target, how strict type checking should be, which files to include) rather than passing flags manually on every `tsc` call - `tsc --init` generates a starter tsconfig.json with sensible defaults and helpful comments. The single most impactful setting worth knowing about early: `\"strict\": true`, which enables TypeScript's full suite of strictness checks (including flagging implicit `any` types and requiring explicit null-handling) - genuinely recommended for any serious project, even though it can feel like 'more work upfront' when you're just starting out.",
