    concept: "Go (often called Golang) was created at Google in 2009, specifically to solve real pain Google's own engineers were experiencing with C++ at massive scale: painfully slow compilation on huge codebases, and dependency management that grew unmanageable across thousands of engineers. Go's core design philosophy is deliberate SIMPLICITY - the language spec is genuinely small (you can read the entire Go spec in an afternoon), with the language designers intentionally leaving out features other modern languages have (no classes/inheritance, no generics until Go 1.18, no exceptions) in favor of a small set of orthogonal, composable primitives.\n\nGo compiles DIRECTLY to a single, statically-linked native binary with no external runtime dependency required (unlike Java needing a JVM, or Python needing an interpreter installed on the target machine) - `go build` produces one executable file you can copy and run anywhere with a matching OS/architecture, genuinely simplifying deployment compared to most other modern languages.\n\nGo today dominates cloud infrastructure specifically: Docker and Kubernetes (the two most foundational tools in modern cloud/DevOps) are BOTH written in Go, and this is precisely why Go skills are in growing demand for backend, cloud infrastructure, and DevOps-adjacent roles - understanding Go often means being able to read and contribute to the actual tools your infrastructure runs on.",
