    whatYoullLearn: ["Installing Rust via rustup", "cargo - Rust's unified build tool, package manager, and project scaffolder", "The difference between cargo run and cargo build"],
    prerequisites: ["Introduction"],
    concept: "Rust is installed via `rustup` (from rust-lang.org), a toolchain installer/manager that also handles updating Rust and switching between versions - `rustc --version` confirms a successful installation. `cargo` (bundled with rustup) is Rust's all-in-one build tool, package manager, and project scaffolder, genuinely central to how virtually all real Rust code is built and shared - parallel in spirit to Go's unified `go` CLI, but with an even heavier emphasis on package management specifically.\n\n`cargo new myproject` scaffolds a complete new project (a `Cargo.toml` manifest file plus a `src/main.rs` starter file); `cargo run` compiles AND runs the project in one step (ideal for development); `cargo build` compiles WITHOUT running, producing an executable in `target/debug/` (or `target/release/` with the `--release` flag, which applies serious optimizations at the cost of longer compile time - genuinely essential for measuring or shipping Rust's real performance, since unoptimized debug builds can be dramatically slower).\n\nExternal dependencies ('crates', Rust's term for packages) are added to `Cargo.toml`'s `[dependencies]` section and automatically downloaded from crates.io (Rust's central package registry, genuinely more centralized than Go's decentralized approach) the next time you run `cargo build` or `cargo run`.",
    keyPoints: ["rustup installs and manages the Rust toolchain; cargo is Rust's unified build tool, package manager, and project scaffolder", "cargo new creates a new project with Cargo.toml and src/main.rs; cargo run compiles+runs; cargo build compiles only, without running", "cargo build --release applies serious optimizations, essential for measuring real performance or shipping production code", "External dependencies ('crates') are declared in Cargo.toml and fetched automatically from crates.io, Rust's central package registry"],
    commonMistakes: ["Measuring or comparing Rust's performance using an unoptimized debug build instead of a --release build, getting misleadingly slow results", "Manually editing files without understanding cargo new's generated project structure (Cargo.toml plus src/main.rs) as the standard starting point"],
