    prerequisites: ["Concurrency", "Traits & Generics", "Lifetimes"],
    concept: "Systems-programming and infrastructure interviews for Rust roles overwhelmingly center on OWNERSHIP - genuinely more than any single topic in any other language path in this course, since it's Rust's entire reason for existing (from the Introduction lesson): 'walk me through what happens when you assign one String variable to another' tests whether you genuinely understand move semantics, not just whether you've memorized the term.\n\nThe handful of topics worth having ROCK-SOLID, in roughly descending order of how often they're actually asked: the three ownership rules and move semantics with a concrete example; the borrowing rule (one mutable XOR many immutable) and why it prevents data races; `Option<T>`/`Result<T,E>` and why Rust has no null; when an explicit lifetime annotation is actually needed and what it means; and the difference between Rust's compile-time-only concurrency safety and Go's optional runtime race-detector tool.\n\nThe same 'definition, example, trade-off' structure from earlier paths applies, but strong Rust answers additionally connect each topic back to a SPECIFIC bug class it eliminates (explaining ownership by naming double-free specifically, borrowing by naming data races specifically, Option by naming null-pointer dereferences specifically) - this demonstrates you understand Rust's actual DESIGN PURPOSE (per the Introduction lesson), not just its mechanical rules in isolation.",
