    prerequisites: ["Syntax"],
    concept: "Kotlin has exactly two variable-declaration keywords, and the choice between them is Kotlin's OWN version of the immutability-first philosophy this course's Rust path introduced: `val` (VALUE, immutable - can only be assigned ONCE) and `var` (VARIABLE, mutable - can be reassigned freely). `val age = 20` cannot be reassigned afterward (`age = 21` is a compile error); `var count = 0; count++` is valid, since `count` was declared mutable.\n\nKotlin has strong TYPE INFERENCE, similar to Go's `:=` or TypeScript's inference: `val age = 20` infers `Int` automatically; explicit type annotations (`val age: Int = 20`) are needed mainly when the type isn't obvious from the initializer, or for a variable declared without an initial value (`var name: String` - Kotlin requires an explicit type here, since there's no initializer to infer from).\n\nThe idiomatic Kotlin guideline, directly parallel to what this course's Rust and TypeScript paths already established: default to `val` for everything, reaching for `var` only when a specific variable genuinely needs to change value over its lifetime - this default-to-immutable habit is considered core, idiomatic Kotlin style (heavily emphasized in Android's own official Kotlin style guide), not an optional nicety.",
    keyPoints: ["val declares an immutable variable (assign-once); var declares a mutable one (can be reassigned freely) - Kotlin's own version of immutability-first design", "Kotlin has strong type inference (val age = 20 infers Int automatically); explicit annotations are needed mainly without an initializer or when the type isn't obvious", "The idiomatic guideline: default to val everywhere, use var only when a variable genuinely needs to change - heavily emphasized in official Android/Kotlin style guides"],
    commonMistakes: ["Using var by default out of habit from languages without this val/var distinction, missing Kotlin's genuine immutability-first idiom", "Trying to reassign a val variable, hitting a compile error that's actually working exactly as intended"],
    interviewTips: ["Be ready to explain the val-versus-var distinction and why defaulting to val is considered idiomatic Kotlin style - a very commonly asked, foundational Kotlin question, especially in Android interviews"],
