    whatYoullLearn: ["Kotlin's core data types, unified around real objects (unlike Java's primitives)", "Nullable types - a first look, expanded fully in the Null Safety lesson", "Type conversion, and why Kotlin has no implicit numeric widening"],
    prerequisites: ["Variables (val/var)"],
    concept: "Kotlin's core types (`Int`, `Long`, `Float`, `Double`, `Boolean`, `Char`, `String`) look similar to Java's, but with one genuine conceptual simplification: EVERYTHING in Kotlin is treated as an object (no separate 'primitive' versus 'wrapper class' distinction the way Java has `int` versus `Integer`) - the Kotlin compiler still optimizes down to Java's efficient primitives under the hood where possible, but you as a developer never need to think about that distinction or manage boxing/unboxing yourself.\n\nEvery Kotlin type is NON-NULLABLE by default: `val age: Int = 20` can NEVER hold `null` - attempting `val age: Int = null` is a COMPILE ERROR. To allow a value to genuinely be absent, you add `?` to the type: `val age: Int? = null` - this `?`-suffix distinction (previewed here, covered in full depth in the dedicated Null Safety lesson) is precisely Kotlin's headline fix for Java's `NullPointerException` problem mentioned back in the Introduction lesson.\n\nLike Rust (and unlike C/C++/Java), Kotlin has NO implicit numeric widening conversion between differently-sized types: `val x: Int = 5; val y: Long = x` is a COMPILE ERROR - you must explicitly convert with `x.toLong()`. This is a deliberate consistency choice (every conversion looks the same, explicit, regardless of whether data could theoretically be lost), directly echoing the same design philosophy Go and Rust both apply to numeric conversions in this course.",
    keyPoints: ["Kotlin treats everything as an object (no Java-style primitive-vs-wrapper distinction) while still compiling to efficient JVM primitives under the hood", "Every Kotlin type is NON-NULLABLE by default - val age: Int = null is a compile error; adding ? (Int?) explicitly allows null", "This ?-suffix nullable-type distinction is Kotlin's headline fix for Java's NullPointerException problem, expanded fully in the Null Safety lesson", "Kotlin has no implicit numeric widening (Int to Long requires explicit .toLong()) - the same deliberate explicitness philosophy as Go and Rust"],
    commonMistakes: ["Trying to assign null to a plain (non-nullable) type without the ? suffix, hitting an immediate, genuinely intentional compile error", "Assuming numeric types convert implicitly between sizes (like Int to Long), when Kotlin requires an explicit .toLong()/.toInt() conversion call"],
    interviewTips: ["Be ready to explain the plain-type-versus-nullable-type (Int vs Int?) distinction as Kotlin's core NullPointerException fix - THE single most commonly asked, most important Kotlin-specific interview topic"],
    realWorldApplications: ["Non-nullable-by-default types are relied upon throughout the entire Kotlin/Android ecosystem to catch an enormous share of what would otherwise be runtime NullPointerException crashes, directly at compile time instead"],
    codeExample: { language: "kotlin", code: "fun main() {\n    val age: Int = 20\n    // val badAge: Int = null  // compile error - Int is non-nullable\n\n    val nickname: String? = null  // explicitly nullable - valid\n\n    val x: Int = 5\n    val y: Long = x.toLong()  // explicit conversion required\n    println(\"$age $nickname $y\")\n}" },
