    prerequisites: ["Conditional Statements"],
    concept: "Kotlin's `for` loop is genuinely built entirely around iterating over something - there's no classic C-style three-part `for (i=0; i<5; i++)` syntax at all; instead, Kotlin uses RANGES: `for (i in 1..5) { ... }` iterates `i` from 1 to 5 INCLUSIVE - `..` creates a range object, and `for...in` iterates it directly, genuinely similar in spirit to Python's `range()`-based for loops or the JS path's `for...of`.\n\n`until` creates a range EXCLUDING the upper bound: `for (i in 0 until 5)` iterates 0,1,2,3,4 (five values, matching a classic zero-indexed 'loop n times' pattern); `downTo` counts DOWNWARD: `for (i in 5 downTo 1)`; `step` changes the increment: `for (i in 0..10 step 2)` iterates 0,2,4,6,8,10 - these range-modifier keywords compose together fluently and read almost like natural English, a genuinely deliberate Kotlin readability choice.\n\n`while` and `do-while` work exactly as expected, identical to Java/C-family languages. Kotlin's `for` can also iterate directly over a COLLECTION (`for (item in myList) { ... }`), the SAME iteration syntax used for ranges - Kotlin deliberately unifies 'loop over a range of numbers' and 'loop over a collection's elements' under one consistent `for...in` syntax, rather than needing separate mental models for each.",
    keyPoints: ["Kotlin's for loop has no classic C-style three-part syntax - it's entirely built around ranges: for (i in 1..5) iterates INCLUSIVE of both ends", "until creates a range EXCLUDING the upper bound (0 until 5 = 0,1,2,3,4); downTo counts downward; step changes the increment", "while/do-while work exactly like Java/C-family languages", "The same for...in syntax used for ranges also iterates directly over collections - one unified mental model for both"],
    commonMistakes: ["Using 1..5 when an exclusive upper bound was actually intended (use 'until' instead), causing an off-by-one iteration count", "Looking for a classic C-style three-part for loop, when Kotlin deliberately builds its for loop entirely around ranges/iterables instead"],
    interviewTips: ["Be ready to explain the difference between .. (inclusive range) and 'until' (exclusive range) with a concrete example - a commonly asked, genuinely important Kotlin-specific distinction"],
    realWorldApplications: ["Range-based for loops are used constantly throughout real Kotlin code for both numeric iteration and collection iteration under one consistent syntax"],
    codeExample: { language: "kotlin", code: "fun main() {\n    for (i in 1..5) print(\"$i \")       // 1 2 3 4 5\n    println()\n    for (i in 0 until 5) print(\"$i \")   // 0 1 2 3 4\n    println()\n    for (i in 10 downTo 0 step 2) print(\"$i \")  // 10 8 6 4 2 0\n    println()\n\n    val fruits = listOf(\"apple\", \"banana\")\n    for (fruit in fruits) println(fruit)  // same for...in syntax\n}" },
