    prerequisites: ["Loops"],
    concept: "Kotlin functions use `fun name(params): ReturnType { ... }`. For a function whose body is just ONE expression, Kotlin offers a genuinely concise shorthand: `fun square(x: Int): Int = x * x` (using `=` instead of `{ return ... }`) - the return type can even be OMITTED here and inferred: `fun square(x: Int) = x * x`, directly parallel to the Rust path's implicit-last-expression-as-return-value idea, just expressed with explicit `=` syntax instead.\n\nKotlin supports DEFAULT ARGUMENTS (`fun greet(name: String, greeting: String = \"Hello\")`) exactly like several other languages in this course, PLUS genuinely useful NAMED ARGUMENTS: `greet(name = \"Priya\", greeting = \"Welcome\")` - calling with explicit parameter names lets you pass arguments in ANY order and makes a call with many parameters dramatically more self-documenting/readable at the call site, especially valuable when several parameters share the same type (like several Int/Boolean flags) where positional-only calling would be genuinely error-prone and ambiguous to read.\n\nKotlin functions are FIRST-CLASS values (same core idea as JavaScript/Go), and Kotlin has full LAMBDA syntax: `val double = { x: Int -> x * 2 }` - a lambda taking an Int and returning `x * 2`, callable as `double(5)`. Higher-order functions (accepting a function as a parameter) are used pervasively, especially through Kotlin's rich COLLECTION methods (covered in the very next lesson): `numbers.filter { it > 10 }` uses `it` as an implicit, automatically-available name for a lambda's single parameter when no explicit name is given.",
    keyPoints: ["Single-expression functions use = instead of { return ... }: fun square(x: Int) = x * x, with the return type often inferable", "Named arguments (greet(name = \"Priya\", greeting = \"Welcome\")) let you pass arguments in any order and self-document a call site, especially valuable with several same-typed parameters", "Kotlin functions are first-class values with full lambda syntax: val double = { x: Int -> x * 2 }", "'it' is the implicit name for a lambda's single parameter when no explicit name is given (numbers.filter { it > 10 })"],
    commonMistakes: ["Writing a full { return ... } body for a function whose logic is a single expression, missing Kotlin's more idiomatic = shorthand", "Overusing 'it' in a lambda complex enough that an explicit parameter name would actually be clearer for readers"],
    interviewTips: ["Be ready to write a lambda using Kotlin's implicit 'it' syntax, and explain named arguments' readability benefit with several same-typed parameters - both commonly asked, practical Kotlin questions"],
