    concept: "C# (pronounced 'C sharp') was created by Microsoft, led by Anders Hejlsberg, and released alongside the original .NET Framework in 2000 - designed deliberately as Microsoft's answer to Java (this course's Java path), sharing a genuinely similar heritage: both are statically-typed, both compile to an intermediate bytecode-like format run by a managed runtime (Java's JVM versus C#'s CLR - Common Language Runtime), and both have automatic garbage collection, meaning most of what you already know from studying Java in this course transfers directly to C# with just syntax differences.\n\n.NET itself has evolved dramatically: the original Windows-only .NET Framework has been succeeded by modern, CROSS-PLATFORM .NET (renamed simply '.NET' from .NET Core onward) - C# code today runs natively on Windows, macOS, and Linux, a genuine shift from its historical Windows-only reputation that's worth knowing if you've heard older, outdated claims about C# being tied to Windows specifically.\n\nC# is genuinely dominant in two, somewhat different domains: ENTERPRISE backend development (ASP.NET Core, Microsoft's web framework, is widely used at large companies), and GAME DEVELOPMENT specifically through Unity (one of the two most popular game engines worldwide, using C# as its primary scripting language) - this dual relevance (enterprise backend AND games) is genuinely distinctive among the languages in this course.",
