    concept: "C#'s `for`, `while`, and `do-while` loops work exactly like Java/C++'s (this course's own C++ path's range-based for loop actually inspired similar features in many later languages, C# included) - the classic three-part `for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)` form is fully supported and commonly used when the index itself is needed.\n\n`foreach` is C#'s idiomatic collection-iteration loop, directly parallel to C++'s range-based for or JavaScript's `for...of`: `foreach (int score in scores) { Console.WriteLine(score); }` - works over ANY type implementing `IEnumerable` (arrays, `List<T>`, and most collection types), and is the preferred, idiomatic choice whenever you need every element in order and don't need the index itself.\n\n`break` and `continue` behave identically to their Java/C++ counterparts covered elsewhere in this course - `break` exits the loop entirely, `continue` skips to the next iteration - no genuinely new behavior here beyond what should already be familiar if you've studied those earlier paths.",
    keyPoints: ["C#'s for/while/do-while work like Java/C++'s; foreach is the idiomatic collection-iteration loop, directly parallel to C++'s range-based for or JS's for...of", "foreach works over any IEnumerable type (arrays, List<T>, most collections) and is preferred when you need every element in order without the index", "break/continue behave identically to their Java/C++ counterparts - no new behavior to learn here if those paths are already familiar"],
    commonMistakes: ["Using a classic indexed for loop out of habit when foreach would be clearer and more idiomatic for straightforward, full-collection iteration", "Trying to modify a collection's structure (adding/removing elements) while iterating it with foreach, which throws an InvalidOperationException"],
    interviewTips: ["Be ready to explain when foreach is preferred over a classic for loop (full iteration, no index needed) versus when the index-based for loop is still the right choice"],
