    prerequisites: ["Loops"],
    concept: "C# arrays (`int[] scores = new int[5];` or `int[] scores = {85, 90, 78};`) are fixed-size, zero-indexed, and DO perform bounds checking (unlike C's raw arrays covered in this course's C path) - accessing an out-of-bounds index throws an `IndexOutOfRangeException` immediately, rather than silently corrupting memory, a genuine safety improvement C shares in common with Java/C++'s std::vector `.at()` (though C#'s plain array indexer always checks, with no separate unchecked variant needed).\n\nFor a DYNAMICALLY-SIZED collection, `List<T>` (from `System.Collections.Generic`) is C#'s equivalent of C++'s `std::vector` or Java's `ArrayList`: `List<int> scores = new List<int>(); scores.Add(85);` - `.Add()`, `.Remove()`, `.Count` (property, not a method call, unlike C++'s `.size()`) round out the essential operations, and in practice, `List<T>` is used far more often than raw fixed-size arrays throughout real C# code, exactly paralleling this course's C++ path's own vector-over-raw-array guidance.\n\nC# also supports genuine MULTI-DIMENSIONAL arrays (`int[,] grid = new int[3, 3];`, a true rectangular 2D array) and JAGGED arrays (`int[][] jagged = new int[3][];`, an array OF arrays, where each inner array can have a DIFFERENT length) - these are genuinely different structures, worth distinguishing: a multi-dimensional array is one single rectangular block of memory; a jagged array is a collection of independently-sized arrays, each potentially stored in a completely different memory location.",
    keyPoints: ["C# arrays are fixed-size and zero-indexed, but DO perform bounds checking (IndexOutOfRangeException on invalid access) - a genuine safety improvement over C's raw arrays", "List<T> is C#'s dynamic array (like C++'s vector or Java's ArrayList) - .Add()/.Remove()/.Count - and is used far more often than raw arrays in real code", "Multi-dimensional arrays (int[,]) are one single rectangular block; jagged arrays (int[][]) are an array of independently-sized arrays - genuinely different structures"],
    commonMistakes: ["Confusing a multi-dimensional array (int[,], one rectangular block) with a jagged array (int[][], an array of independently-sized arrays) - they look similar but are structurally different", "Using a fixed-size array when the needed size isn't known upfront, instead of just using List<T>"],
    interviewTips: ["Be ready to explain the difference between a multi-dimensional array and a jagged array precisely - a commonly asked, genuinely distinguishing C#-specific question"],
    realWorldApplications: ["List<T> is the single most commonly used collection type throughout virtually all real-world C# code, used far more often than raw fixed-size arrays in typical application logic"],
    codeExample: { language: "csharp", code: "using System;\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\n\nint[] scores = { 85, 90, 78 };\n// scores[10] would throw IndexOutOfRangeException\n\nList<int> dynamicScores = new List<int>();\ndynamicScores.Add(85);\ndynamicScores.Add(90);\nConsole.WriteLine(dynamicScores.Count);\n\nint[,] grid = new int[2, 2] { { 1, 2 }, { 3, 4 } };  // multi-dimensional\nConsole.WriteLine(grid[1, 1]);  // 4" },
