    prerequisites: ["Strings"],
    concept: "C# methods use `returnType MethodName(params) { ... }` - method OVERLOADING (multiple methods sharing a name with different parameter signatures) works exactly like Java/C++'s equivalent feature covered elsewhere in this course.\n\nC# passes arguments BY VALUE by default (exactly like Java/C++), but provides two explicit keywords for modifying a caller's variable directly: `ref` (the variable must ALREADY be initialized before the call, and the method can both read AND modify it) and `out` (previewed briefly in the Input Output lesson - the variable does NOT need to be initialized beforehand, since the method is expected to ALWAYS assign it) - both require the SAME keyword at both the parameter declaration AND the call site (`void Increment(ref int x)` called as `Increment(ref age);`), making the by-reference behavior visible and unambiguous right at the call site itself, unlike C++'s reference parameters (covered in this course's C++ path) which need no special syntax at the call site at all.\n\nC# supports DEFAULT/OPTIONAL PARAMETERS (`void Greet(string name, string greeting = \"Hello\")`) and NAMED ARGUMENTS (`Greet(greeting: \"Welcome\", name: \"Priya\")`), directly parallel to the exact same features already covered in this course's Kotlin and Python paths - genuinely useful for the same readability and flexible-ordering reasons covered there.",
    keyPoints: ["C# methods support overloading (same name, different parameters), and pass arguments by value by default, like Java/C++", "ref requires the variable be initialized before the call and lets the method read+modify it; out doesn't require prior initialization since the method must always assign it", "Both ref and out require the SAME keyword at both the parameter declaration and the call site, making by-reference behavior visible right at the call site (unlike C++'s reference parameters)", "Default/optional parameters and named arguments work directly parallel to this course's Kotlin/Python paths - same readability and ordering-flexibility benefits"],
    commonMistakes: ["Forgetting to write 'ref'/'out' at the CALL SITE (not just the method declaration) - C# requires both, unlike C++'s reference parameters which need no call-site marker at all", "Using 'ref' when 'out' would be more appropriate (or vice versa) - ref implies the variable already has a meaningful value the method might use; out implies the method will always assign a fresh value regardless of any prior content"],
