    realWorldApplications: ["The out parameter pattern (seen already with int.TryParse) is used pervasively throughout the real C#/.NET standard library for methods that need to return both a success/failure status AND an actual value"],
    codeExample: { language: "csharp", code: "using System;\n\nvoid Increment(ref int x)\n{\n    x++;\n}\n\nvoid Square(int input, out int result)\n{\n    result = input * input;  // must always assign 'out' parameters\n}\n\nvoid Greet(string name, string greeting = \"Hello\")\n{\n    Console.WriteLine($\"{greeting}, {name}!\");\n}\n\nint age = 20;\nIncrement(ref age);\nConsole.WriteLine(age);  // 21\n\nSquare(5, out int squared);\nConsole.WriteLine(squared);  // 25\n\nGreet(greeting: \"Welcome\", name: \"Priya\");  // named arguments" },
