    concept: "C# classes work similarly to Java/C++'s (`class Student { private string name; public Student(string name) { this.name = name; } }`), but C# has a genuinely distinctive, heavily-used feature: PROPERTIES - a concise syntax that looks like a plain field from the OUTSIDE, but can run actual GET/SET logic internally: `public int Age { get; set; }` (an 'auto-property', generating a hidden backing field automatically) versus a fuller form with actual validation logic: `public int Age { get { return _age; } set { _age = value >= 0 ? value : 0; } }` - `value` is an automatically-available implicit parameter name inside a `set` block, representing whatever was assigned.\n\nThis directly replaces the verbose `getAge()`/`setAge(int age)` method-pair pattern this course's Java/C++ paths would otherwise require - callers write `student.Age = 25;` and `int a = student.Age;`, syntactically IDENTICAL to accessing a plain field, while the class author retains full control to validate or compute the value behind the scenes - genuinely one of C#'s most distinctive, most heavily-used ergonomic improvements over Java's convention-based getter/setter approach.\n\nAccess modifiers (`private`, `public`, `protected`, plus C#'s own `internal` - accessible anywhere within the SAME assembly/project, but not from an external one) work similarly to Java/C++'s equivalent concepts, controlling encapsulation the same way covered in this course's other OOP-focused paths.",
