    goingDeeper: "`Task.WhenAll(task1, task2, task3)` runs multiple Tasks CONCURRENTLY and completes once ALL of them finish - directly parallel to JavaScript's `Promise.all()` from this course's JS path - genuinely useful when several independent async operations don't depend on each other and awaiting them one at a time sequentially would waste time unnecessarily.",
