    prerequisites: [],
    concept: "Bash (Bourne Again SHell) is the default command-line SHELL on most Linux systems and macOS (until recently) - a program whose entire job is reading commands you type and executing them, either interactively (one command at a time in a terminal) or as a SCRIPT (a saved file of many commands run in sequence). This is a genuinely different kind of language from everything else in this course: Bash's core purpose isn't general-purpose application logic - it's ORCHESTRATING other programs, files, and processes, gluing together existing command-line tools rather than implementing algorithms from scratch.\n\nBash's most fundamental design idea: EVERYTHING is built around running commands and manipulating TEXT that flows between them - a Bash script is fundamentally a sequence of commands, most of which are actually separate, independent programs (`ls`, `grep`, `cat`) that Bash simply launches and coordinates, not built-in language features the way C's `printf` or Python's `print` are part of the language runtime itself.\n\nEven if your actual day-to-day work is in Python, Java, or JavaScript, Bash scripting is genuinely essential: automating a deployment, writing a CI/CD pipeline step, quickly processing a log file, or setting up a development environment virtually always involves at least some Bash, making it one of the most practically unavoidable skills for any real-world software engineering role, regardless of your primary language.",
