    concept: "A saved Bash script needs to be marked EXECUTABLE before it can be run directly: `chmod +x hello.sh` grants execute permission, then `./hello.sh` runs it (the `./` explicitly means 'in the current directory' - just `hello.sh` alone usually won't work, for a genuinely important reason covered next). Alternatively, `bash hello.sh` runs the script by explicitly invoking bash on it, without needing the execute permission at all - both approaches are common, though `./hello.sh` (with the shebang from the previous lesson) is the more idiomatic, 'this is a real, standalone program' style.\n\nEssential navigation: `pwd` (print working directory) shows where you currently are; `cd directory` changes into it (`cd ..` goes UP one level, `cd ~` goes to your home directory); `ls` lists a directory's contents (`ls -la` shows ALL files including hidden ones, in a detailed LONG format with permissions/sizes/dates).\n\nThe PATH environment variable is WHY typing `ls` alone works but `./hello.sh` alone typically doesn't: PATH is a list of directories the shell automatically SEARCHES for a matching program name when you type a bare command - `ls` lives in one of those PATH directories (like `/bin` or `/usr/bin`), but your own script (in your current working directory) almost certainly doesn't, unless you've deliberately added your current directory to PATH (a genuinely risky practice most systems avoid by default, for security reasons) - which is precisely why an explicit `./` prefix (meaning 'look right here, not in PATH') is needed for your own scripts.",
