    whatYoullLearn: ["Command substitution - capturing a command's output into a variable", "The modern $(...) syntax versus legacy backticks", "Why quoting matters critically once command substitution is involved"],
    prerequisites: ["Variables"],
    concept: "COMMAND SUBSTITUTION captures a command's OUTPUT and uses it as a value, typically stored in a variable: `today=$(date +%Y-%m-%d)` runs the `date` command, captures whatever it printed, and stores that captured text in `today` - genuinely central to Bash scripting, since it's how a script incorporates dynamic, real-time information (the current date, a file count, a running process's ID) rather than only hardcoded values.\n\nThe modern syntax is `$(command)`; an OLDER, legacy syntax uses backticks: `` `command` `` - both do the same thing, but `$(...)` is strongly preferred in all new code since it NESTS cleanly (`$(command1 $(command2))` reads clearly, while nested backticks require awkward escaping) and is visually easier to spot in a longer line of code.\n\nA genuinely critical, frequently-tested gotcha connecting directly back to the file-handling-caution mindset this course has built up: command substitution's captured output should almost ALWAYS be used inside DOUBLE QUOTES: `echo \"$today\"` not `echo $today` - without quotes, Bash performs WORD SPLITTING on the captured value (breaking it into separate 'words' at whitespace) and GLOB EXPANSION (treating `*`/`?` as filename-matching wildcards) - a filename captured via command substitution that happens to contain a space (a genuinely common real-world occurrence) would be silently split into multiple separate arguments without the protecting quotes, causing a command to behave completely differently than intended.",
    keyPoints: ["Command substitution ($(command)) captures a command's OUTPUT for use as a value, typically assigned to a variable", "$(...) is the modern, preferred syntax over legacy backticks (`command`), since it nests cleanly without awkward escaping", "ALWAYS quote a captured value when using it (\"$variable\", not bare $variable) - without quotes, Bash performs word-splitting and glob expansion that can silently break a value containing spaces or special characters"],
    commonMistakes: ["Using legacy backticks instead of $(...) in new scripts, missing the cleaner nesting the modern syntax provides", "Forgetting to quote a captured variable (echo $filename instead of echo \"$filename\"), causing silent, hard-to-debug breakage on any value containing a space or wildcard character"],
