    prerequisites: ["Command Substitution"],
    concept: "Bash's `if` looks structurally similar to other languages but has a genuinely distinctive test syntax: `if [ \"$age\" -ge 18 ]; then echo \"adult\"; elif [ \"$age\" -ge 13 ]; then echo \"teen\"; else echo \"child\"; fi` - `[ ... ]` is actually a COMMAND (literally an alias for the `test` program), not special syntax; `if` just checks whether that command's EXIT CODE was 0 (success) - this is precisely why spaces around the brackets (`[ \"$age\" -ge 18 ]`, not `[\"$age\" -ge 18]`) are MANDATORY, since `[` needs to be recognized as a separate command word, and `fi` (if spelled backward) closes the block.\n\nA genuinely critical, frequently-tested distinction: STRING comparison uses `=`/`!=` (`[ \"$name\" = \"Priya\" ]`); NUMERIC comparison uses entirely different operators - `-eq` (equal), `-ne` (not equal), `-gt`/`-lt` (greater/less than), `-ge`/`-le` (greater/less-or-equal) - `[ \"5\" = \"5.0\" ]` and `[ \"5\" -eq \"5.0\" ]` can genuinely give DIFFERENT results (`=` compares as literal text, `-eq` converts and compares numerically) - using the wrong comparison type for the kind of data you're actually comparing is one of THE most common Bash scripting bugs.\n\n`[ -f \"$filename\" ]` tests whether a file exists (and is a regular file); `[ -d \"$dirname\" ]` tests for a directory; `[ -z \"$var\" ]` tests whether a string is EMPTY; `[ -n \"$var\" ]` tests whether it's NON-empty - these file/string test operators are genuinely central to writing any real, robust Bash script that needs to check its environment before acting on it.",
